V3DRender 0.11: kit 13, frames read back faster
================================================

riscos-mesa asked for two things so that drawing on the GPU isn't slowed
down by reading each frame back from uncached memory:

  - BOs the CPU maps cacheable (V3DRender keeps the caches right around
    each GPU job), and
  - the GPU drawing straight into the program's own memory (a sprite,
    the screen): "importing" it.

Check's new test 11 draws a 1920x1080 frame into each kind - uncached
as before, cached, and imported - after the CPU has read the old
contents (so a missing cache invalidate would show), checks every
pixel, draws a second frame on the same memory, and prints how fast
the CPU reads the frame back. Please send Results: the MB/s figures
are what riscos-mesa needs.

Everything from kit 12 (*V3DReset, HangTest's test 10) is unchanged.

Before you start
- A Pi 4, Pi 400 or CM4 with RISC OS 5.30 or later. V3DDriver not
  loaded. Copy this folder to a hard disc.

The test
1. Double-click Replay. It loads V3DRender 0.09 from this folder, in
   place of an older one. No program should be using the GPU at the same time:
   Replay needs the buffer addresses Mesa's job had.
2. Optional: double-click Check too (kits 1-5's tests, all passed in
   kit 5).
3. Send Results back.
